my lord richard dannet is uh with us now  former chief of the general staff of  course um hello my lord thank you for  joining us on the program this morning  what's your interpretation of what's  happening in ukraine at the moment  well okay we are witnessing some uh  incredible scenes  um the russians undoubtedly have  suffered a significant setback and the  ukrainians have been pretty clever in in  what they've been doing if you remember  over the last few weeks we've been  talking a lot about a possible  counter-offensive in the south around  the area of curson and everyone took  that seriously indeed there had been  counter-offensive moves uh in the south  but it was played up to be the big  counter-offensive such that the russians  deployed some of their better troops  away from the north to counter this a  potential counter-offensive in the south  but in fact what the ukrainians had in  mind all along was actually this major  attack not in the south but actually in  the north in the kharkiv area and that's  what they have unfolded dramatically and  very successfully uh in the in the last  few days and they've pulled off an  operational level surprise um quite a  significant thing to be able to do  result of that is that the russians have  pretty much turned and fled in that area  of ukraine um cupianze which was one of  their major logistic hubs has fallen  many of the railway tracks um bringing  supplies in to the russians converge in  that part of the world and indeed the  town of israel as well they've both  fallen  and this is a significant reverse for  the russians a great success for the  ukrainians and as president zielenski is  saying um they'll do more but they  continue to need more and more western  arms and ammunition in order to be able  to keep the pressure up but tell them  it's been no doubt that this is a  significant uh improvement in the  circumstances great for the people that  um deborah was just talking to but um if  you just look at the map um although the  ukrainians have made significant  advances there's a lot of their country  still in russian occupation um so  there's a long way to go  yeah and as far as the blackouts are  concerned that we've been hearing about  that can cause no end of problems not  only for civilians but also for the  military  well  of course you're right but what we're  seeing is the russians reacting in their  typically heavy-handed way  they know that they've had a significant  reverse on the battlefield so they're  lashing out in other ways to to try and  restore their position  and the attack on the power station  which has reduced that part of the  kharkiv area to darkness uh is a typical  example of them blindly lashing out with  everything they possibly can to try and  reassert themselves but but i think  experience shows looking right back  through history that if you're trying to  win a war by cowing the opposition's  public it just doesn't work  and the resistance and the attitude that  you're seeing from the ukrainian people  is yes no we don't like the darkness we  don't like the lack of water but we will  win through  they will not break our spirit  and i think that is the other critical  issue morale in ukraine morale amongst  the ukrainian forces is sky high when  amongst the russian forces it is  terribly low  and turning and running and fleeing base  hospitals being overrun being evacuated  i mean this is these are signs of well i  was going to say an army in panic  certainly an army in disarray and putin  and his senior generals have got a real  problem to know how to stabilize the  area um in the north they'll do it  because their numbers are such that they  will but um  there's no getting away from the fact  they've suffered a major reverse at the  present moment  uh but putin is i suppose potentially it  is most dangerous when he is cornered  and thus the same for his army  you're right and i think you're probably  suggesting um the question of  will he up the ante um possibly even in  a nuclear way well  i would like  fervently hope that he will not do that  i think he has other options  um he's always called this a special  military operation and has tried to use  in-place forces he has got the option if  he thinks politically he could get away  with it by declaring war  with the change to regulations and  change the law in  russia that that would bring whereby he  could bring in conscription he could  start to mobilize other parts of the  russian military to try and stabilize  the front but um  there's great political risks in him  doing that so far he's managed to  contain what the russian people know  about this appalling  operation that he began  but if he goes down  down the track  of calling it a war  fully mobilizing his country bringing in  conscription then the political stakes  for him become increasingly high and  maybe he won't do that  president zelinski has said the next  three months are critical as we head  into the winter time would you agree  with him  uh yes i think actually every day is  critical every week is critical at the  present moment um the period of maneuver  can continue during what's an  increasingly wet period on the ground  and then as we get closer to the end of  the year uh the temperatures will drop  the ground will freeze  and that'll make conditions very  difficult but it does actually mean that  degree of movement can start again but  um  i think those circumstances are such  that i would expect to see continuing  movement now  and then probably the war going into a  fairly period of stasis over the worst  of the winter and then we'll see what  happens again next spring  because the russians have been trying to  amass a larger force  they've been putting together something  called the third army corps  which is a force that they're trying to  recruit and train and we're hoping to  put back into the field next spring  because of the pressure on them they've  had to put some of those units already  into the field probably only partially  trained so  the thing is spiraling out of beauty  into control  um as i say i think we'll see more  movement in the next few weeks probably  slowing down during the heart of the  winter and then maybe the day new more  next spring  before i let you go um my lord a thought  on the passing of the queen what impact  does it have uh on the military when  there is a new  sovereign  well it has impact on all of us in the  country but i think it has a particular  impact on members of the armed forces um  much has been made of the fact that when  we join the army navy air force or  whatever um  we sign we we swear an oath of  allegiance and i did that on the 28th of  august 1969 a long time ago now and that  oath of allegiance was to queen  elizabeth ii but actually the uh phrase  also her heirs and successors were part  of that oath so seamlessly when the  queen breathed her last last thursday  our allegiance as soldiers of the queen  we immediately became soldiers of the  king and that's a very special link that  the military have we carry out  operations at risk of life and limb not  in the name of the government or the  prime minister or the secretary of state  for defense we do it in the name of the  sovereign and the people of this country  that's a very special link a very  special bond actually i think it makes  the british armed forces themselves  pretty special as a result  indeed so my lord thank you so much for  taking the time to join us on the  program this morning thank you thank you
 

